{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 43, :comments ["<b>And if the plague return and break out in the house</b> — Now this plague that comes in the other stones and in the other mortar is not the first plague, and it is not like the breaking out spoken of regarding a person and the spreading, which returns to its first place in its nature, for many times the moisture hides beneath the skin and retreats into the interior of the body, and afterwards returns and draws toward the skin and its outer parts. But the matter is as I wrote in the section \"When a woman has her discharge\" (Ramban on Leviticus 13:47), that it is a plague and a stroke of Elohim, that there be an evil spirit of YHWH in that place.\n\nAnd in Torat Kohanim (section 7:1) they said: \"And if the plague return and break out in the house\" — a parable: a certain man returned to his place. And the intention is to say that \"and break out\" stated here is not \"spreading,\" as in \"and if the leprosy break out abroad in the skin\" (Leviticus 13:12), but rather that it grows in that place, like the matter of \"the rod of Aaron sprouted\" (Numbers 17:23). And the parable they mentioned hints at the matter we stated, that this is not a plague newly born and spreading but rather growing and returning and growing, like a man who went away and returned to sit in his first place, on a different seat that was prepared for him there. And the reason for \"breaking out\" I have already explained. And likewise tzara'at \"is breaking out\" (Leviticus 13:57) in garments.\n\nAnd Scripture did not say \"and if the plague return and break out in those stones\" but rather even if it appeared in a different place in the house, and even in a different appearance that is not like the appearance of the first plague, it is judged as one that returns and breaks out, and is not judged as a plague newly born from the start. And this is the reason for \"in the house,\" for in the entire house its law is thus. And the reason is that the evil spirit does not depart from that house, but rather it will always be in that house in one of the places, to frighten its owners.\n\nAnd so they said in Torat Kohanim (7:1:3): I have only its place; from where do I include the entire house? Scripture says \"in the house.\" I have only in its appearances; not in its appearances from where? Scripture says \"and break out.\" And the matter of these verses according to the midrash is: if the plague returns on the seventh day, or it returned in the house after he removed the stones and after he scraped and after he plastered — or the priest comes for a second entry and sees, and behold the plague has now spread in the house according to the first judgment — \"it is a plague of leprosy in stubborn malice,\" and he shall demolish the house. This is to say that the spreading in the first instance or the spreading in the second instance, they have one law. And there was no need to say \"and the priest shall come and see and behold the plague has spread in the house and he shall command and they shall remove the stones\" and the entire passage, for Scripture continues from above, to say: if the plague returns after the removal of the stones in the first instance or in the second, when the priest comes and sees the spreading — \"it is a plague of leprosy in stubborn malice.\" And He further said (Leviticus 14:48) \"and if the priest shall come in,\" in the second entry mentioned, \"and see and behold the plague has not spread in the house after the house was plastered,\" then the priest shall purify the house, as the plague has been healed from it — that is to say, it did not return in it.\n\nAnd it is now learned that that which stands unchanged in the first week and in the second — he removes and scrapes and plasters and gives it a week, and if it returned, he demolishes. And this is the proper arrangement of the verses according to their midrash, for it is impossible to cut them with a knife, advancing and delaying something that is not included in their meaning.\n\nAnd it is further possible to say regarding the proper arrangement of this matter, that \"spread\" in this place has the meaning of \"sprouted\" (פָּרַח), and both of them have the meaning of growth. For when there is a plague there, he says \"spread\" concerning its growth and its expansion, and when there is no plague there, he says \"spread\" concerning its growth and its return, for all of it is the matter of growth. And so Onkelos translates all of them as \"will add\" (אוֹסֵיף), and the language of addition may be said of a thing that grows and is added to another, as in \"and he too will be added to our enemies\" (Exodus 1:10), \"and their inheritance will be added\" (Numbers 36:4), and it may also be said of a thing that returns, as in \"YHWH will again set His hand\" (Isaiah 11:11), \"and they prophesied and did not add\" (Numbers 11:25) — they did not return again to prophesy. And behold, its meaning is that the plague returned.\n\nAnd so, after the removal of the stones, when Scripture mentions \"spreading,\" its meaning will be the same as \"sprouting,\" as I mentioned regarding the term \"breaking out.\" And if so, Scripture says: and if the plague returns and sprouts in the house after the stones were removed, and the priest comes and sees and behold the plague has sprouted in the house — he demolishes it, for every plague that returns is definitively impure. And if the priest comes and sees that the plague did not sprout at all in the house after the plastering — he purifies it, for it was healed by the removal and plastering.\n\nAnd so the law of the house regarding spreading in the first instance is explained here: he removes and scrapes and plasters and gives it a week, and if it returned it is impure, and if it did not return it is pure. But regarding that which stands unchanged in the first week and spreads in the second, its law is not explained in the Torah, but we judge it by a gezeirah shavah: this is \"coming\" (בִּיאָה), this is \"returning\" (שִׁיבָה) (Torat Kohanim 7:7), to say that the law of \"coming\" that is stated at the end of the second week is like the law of \"returning\" that is stated at the end of the first week — in both of them, if it spread, he removes and scrapes and plasters and gives it a week.\n\nAnd likewise that which stands unchanged in the first and second weeks and spreads in the third is not mentioned in the Torah, but it is learned (in Torat Kohanim 7:5–10) from another gezeirah shavah, for it says (Leviticus 14:48) \"and if the priest shall come in,\" that a third entry is judged like the second entry. And so the verses are according to their plain meaning, and the midrashic derivations learned them from gezeirah shavah given to Moses at Sinai.\n\nAnd what they said in Torat Kohanim (7:7), \"About what does Scripture speak?\" etc. — this is supported by their words, for they intended to allude in Scripture to the matter received by them through gezeirah shavah. And likewise what they said there (Torat Kohanim 7:6): If we end up including the returning one even though it did not spread, what is the meaning of \"and the priest shall come and see and behold it has spread\"? — leave it as it is. That is to say, we leave the verse according to its plain meaning and follow the midrash — not that we uproot the verse from its place and fix it elsewhere. This is what appears to me regarding the matter of this passage, in order that the words of the Sages be established, and it is a fine and acceptable thing."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The cross-reference 'בסדר אשה כי תזריע' refers to Ramban's commentary on Leviticus 13:47, which falls in the Torah portion Tazria beginning at Leviticus 12:2 with the words 'אשה כי תזריע'."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:54:28.612743828Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}